        "target": "Given that Kate initially has 2 red balloons and 4 blue balloons, and then inflates 4 more balloons, 2 of each color, I first identified the total number of red and blue balloons after the inflation. Initially, there are 2 red balloons, and after inflating 2 more red balloons, the total number of red balloons becomes 2 + 2 = 4. Similarly, the total number of blue balloons becomes 4 + 2 = 6. The total number of balloons is now 4 red + 6 blue = 10 balloons.\n\nTo find the percent likelihood that a randomly selected balloon will be red, I used the formula for percentage:\n\\[ \\text{Percent likelihood} = \\left( \\frac{\\text{Number of red balloons}}{\\text{Total number of balloons}} \\right) \\times 100 \\]\n\nSubstituting the numbers:\n\\[ \\text{Percent likelihood} = \\left( \\frac{4}{10} \\right) \\times 100 = 40\\% \\]\n\nI checked this result by ensuring that the calculation was correct and that the percentage represented the proportion of red balloons out of the total number of balloons. The operation I used is directly applicable to similar problems where the probability of selecting a specific type of item from a collection is required.\n\nI would reuse this operation whenever I need to calculate the probability of selecting a specific item from a collection, given the total number of items and the number of items of the specific type.\n\nFINAL: 40",
